Clarification on Jira Ownership and Process

I want to take a moment to clarify the process around the Jira we are working on and why I suggested that I continue with the implementation.

For every business requirement, a Jira story is created and, by design, it can only be assigned to one developer. If a requirement is large or complex, it can be broken down into multiple Jiras or subtasks, but one Jira remains assigned to one developer from start to finish. Our leadership has also given clear guidance that any code a developer works on should be tied to a Jira assigned to their name, so that accountability and tracking are consistent.

In this case, the Jira is assigned to me. I have been involved from the beginning, gathering requirements, working on the initial changes, and now preparing for the latest update. My responsibility is to see this Jira through end to end: implementing the code, coordinating with QA, answering queries, and supporting the release.

When work on a Jira shifts between developers mid-way, it causes unnecessary context switching and dilutes ownership. The developer who writes the code has the full context of why it was written in a certain way, and is best placed to test it thoroughly and take responsibility for it in case of future issues. If another developer steps in mid-way, even with good intentions, there is always a higher chance of gaps or misunderstandings that can lead to production defects. Of course, there are exceptions, such as if a developer is on extended leave, but otherwise the standard should be that one Jira is owned by one developer throughout.

Another important point is around requirement gathering. When you attended the meeting with the business team, you shared the new requirements with me on the call, which was helpful. But as I mentioned earlier, requirements cannot remain only in verbal form. They need to be documented in writing, and then reflected in Jira. Jira must always be the single source of truth that developers implement from and QA tests against. If requirements are discussed verbally but not updated in Jira, it creates gaps and leads to “he said, she said” situations later if defects occur. Written requirements ensure accountability, eliminate ambiguity, and provide a clear reference for everyone involved. The organization expects us to follow this process so that work is not rushed or handled informally. Cutting corners on process may feel faster in the short term, but eventually it leads to defects and rework.

I also want to mention briefly that something similar happened earlier during the September release, when we had to integrate the new BOSS health check into the router. At that time, I had been coordinating with the BOSS team, gathering requirements, testing their new URL, and discussing timelines with Amit. But before the work could be finalized, you picked up and implemented the change. That was not your fault since we had never clarified the process then, but it highlights why we should avoid switching ownership mid-way. It may happen once, but we should not make it a recurring practice. Going forward, we should stick to the agreed process.

So, in short, I will continue to take ownership of this Jira since it is assigned to me, and you will continue with the Jiras assigned to you. That way, each of us remains accountable for our work and there is no ambiguity.

I also want to acknowledge openly that the distribution of work between onshore and offshore is not always even. Onshore teammates often receive more development tasks simply because of the proximity to business and being in the same time zone. This is not something you personally need to act on, but I think it is worth recognizing as a reality. When needed, this can be brought up in the right forums.

If you feel differently about this approach, I encourage you to raise it with \[Team Lead’s Name]. I am happy to discuss it together so that we can all align on the right way forward.

Thanks for understanding.
